WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.030 --> 00:00:01.520
The field of historical archaeology can be

00:00:01.520 --> 00:00:04.120
predominantly white and male.

00:00:04.120 --> 00:00:06.440
Coming into this field if you actually don't see

00:00:06.440 --> 00:00:09.300
yourself in it, don't see your research

00:00:09.300 --> 00:00:11.700
questions as valued--why would you

00:00:11.700 --> 00:00:14.108
why would you even attempt it?

00:00:14.108 --> 00:00:17.120
Justin and I really wanted to create a space where

00:00:17.120 --> 00:00:20.189
we valued our experiences in this field

00:00:20.189 --> 00:00:22.160
We valued the knowledge backgrounds that

00:00:22.160 --> 00:00:24.800
we come with to this practice.

00:00:24.800 --> 00:00:26.740
The Slave Wrecks project really provided the seed

00:00:26.740 --> 00:00:29.300
funding, not just for the scuba diving training

00:00:29.310 --> 00:00:31.760
but also for the terrestrial archaeological work

00:00:31.760 --> 00:00:33.820
that we do here at the Estate Little Princes.

00:00:33.820 --> 00:00:36.220
So what began as a sort of one-week youth training

00:00:36.220 --> 00:00:39.680
program, has spun out into a one-week youth training

00:00:39.680 --> 00:00:41.940
program, there's four weeks of archaeology,

00:00:41.940 --> 00:00:45.160
and now we're up to seven HBCU students all doing

00:00:45.160 --> 00:00:47.920
archaeology together at this site.

00:00:48.420 --> 00:00:50.680
The undergraduates that are with us this year--

00:00:50.680 --> 00:00:52.560
we have folks from Morgan State,

00:00:52.560 --> 00:00:53.760
we have folks from Spellman,

00:00:53.760 --> 00:00:55.770
we have folks from Howard University, and

00:00:55.770 --> 00:00:59.144
it's a lot of energy. There's a lot of excitement.

00:00:59.144 --> 00:01:00.899
There's a lot of curiosity.

00:01:00.899 --> 00:01:02.800
These are students who are coming from

00:01:02.800 --> 00:01:06.689
institutions that don't have archaeology

00:01:06.689 --> 00:01:09.740
as a core class that they could take,

00:01:09.740 --> 00:01:13.560
so for a lot of them it's  their first experience in this field,

00:01:13.560 --> 00:01:16.590
and they have gotten a crash course on

00:01:16.590 --> 00:01:19.820
archaeological theory, method and now they're out here

00:01:19.820 --> 00:01:23.020
in the field really digging with us.

00:01:23.020 --> 00:01:25.680
And in the mix of all of that we're also asking them,

00:01:25.680 --> 00:01:28.100
"What are you curious about?"

00:01:28.100 --> 00:01:30.100
And often times it's that thought,

00:01:30.100 --> 00:01:31.560
and that point of curiosity

00:01:31.560 --> 00:01:33.549
that'll lead to greater research questions

00:01:33.549 --> 00:01:38.840
and a desire to have those questions answered.

00:01:38.840 --> 00:01:42.640
Since we have five PhDs leading this initiative,

00:01:42.640 --> 00:01:45.140
that means that we can literally open a pipeline

00:01:45.140 --> 00:01:47.560
that's never been attempted before,

00:01:47.560 --> 00:01:49.940
where we can have Dr. Alexandra Jones working with

00:01:49.940 --> 00:01:51.520
our middle school and high school students

00:01:51.520 --> 00:01:52.260
getting training.

00:01:52.260 --> 00:01:54.260
We can have Dr. Flewellen and Dr. Dunnavant

00:01:54.260 --> 00:01:56.620
working with HBCU students from across the country

00:01:56.620 --> 00:01:58.460
at the undergraduate level getting training,

00:01:58.460 --> 00:02:01.440
and then I am now working with graduate students

00:02:01.440 --> 00:02:03.160
that are coming into the University of Tulsa

00:02:03.160 --> 00:02:06.460
to get additional artifact analysis training in artifacts

00:02:06.460 --> 00:02:08.640
that are coming out of Estate Little Princess.

00:02:08.640 --> 00:02:11.760
So really from middle school to graduate school,

00:02:11.760 --> 00:02:14.020
we have this open pipeline to where we're seeing

00:02:14.020 --> 00:02:17.040
students get access to archaeological training,

00:02:17.040 --> 00:02:21.000
artifact analysis training, and underwater training as well,

00:02:21.000 --> 00:02:23.520
all in one program, for free.

00:02:23.520 --> 00:02:25.920
So when we all came together one of the first questions

00:02:25.920 --> 00:02:27.300
that we asked each other were

00:02:27.300 --> 00:02:29.420
"Alright what did you love about your field school

00:02:29.420 --> 00:02:32.340
experiences, and what was challenging about them?

00:02:32.340 --> 00:02:33.666
And how can we address that?"

00:02:33.666 --> 00:02:35.760
And that goes from like the logistics of like what

00:02:35.760 --> 00:02:38.340
paperwork we have, but also thinking

00:02:38.340 --> 00:02:40.840
about, "Alright what sort of experiences

00:02:40.840 --> 00:02:43.120
do we want to make sure that these students have while

00:02:43.120 --> 00:02:43.920
down here?"

00:02:43.920 --> 00:02:46.530
And we don't want to work them into the ground,

00:02:46.530 --> 00:02:49.500
We understand that yes this is a field site,

00:02:49.500 --> 00:02:51.760
and we have our research questions,

00:02:51.760 --> 00:02:55.700
and this is a space to educate people.

00:02:55.700 --> 00:02:58.980
So this really has become a training site for many of us,

00:02:58.980 --> 00:03:01.140
and not just for the students as well it's also for the

00:03:01.140 --> 00:03:04.240
researchers. So anytime we talk about setting up a unit

00:03:04.240 --> 00:03:06.460
to do excavations, we have to make sure that all

00:03:06.460 --> 00:03:07.520
five of us are on board.

00:03:07.520 --> 00:03:09.920
Anytime we talk about how we're gonna classify

00:03:09.920 --> 00:03:12.320
artifacts, we need to make sure that all of us on board.

00:03:12.320 --> 00:03:14.440
So it's very much a slow-moving process,

00:03:14.440 --> 00:03:16.920
but I think it makes us all better as researchers.

00:03:16.920 --> 00:03:19.280
And then when we go split off and do other projects,

00:03:19.280 --> 00:03:21.940
maybe around the island or in other sites,

00:03:21.940 --> 00:03:23.520
we know that we have a foundation

00:03:23.520 --> 00:03:25.520
by which we can build upon.

00:03:25.520 --> 00:03:28.540
Archaeology really needs to be more community-based,

00:03:28.540 --> 00:03:30.840
and my colleague Dr. Ayana Flewellen and I have

00:03:30.840 --> 00:03:32.660
been talking about sustainable archaeology,

00:03:32.660 --> 00:03:35.560
and sustainability in the sense that the projects and the

00:03:35.560 --> 00:03:38.420
history, and the work can continue to go on well after we

00:03:38.420 --> 00:03:41.580
leave, but also sustainability in recognizing that the

00:03:41.580 --> 00:03:43.620
archaeological work that we do is destructive,

00:03:43.620 --> 00:03:46.000
and if we're going to destructive work,

00:03:46.000 --> 00:03:48.860
we need to also do some sort of restitution

00:03:48.860 --> 00:03:51.240
to the environment that we're destroying.

00:03:51.240 --> 00:03:54.480
And so having students and having ourselves trained

00:03:54.480 --> 00:03:56.380
in a way where we're actually able to do both

00:03:56.380 --> 00:03:58.900
the conservation work and the archaeological work,

00:03:58.900 --> 00:04:01.360
really makes it that much more important,

00:04:01.360 --> 00:04:03.640
and that much more impactful when we enter a space

00:04:03.640 --> 00:04:06.220
and begin to think about doing research there.

00:04:06.220 --> 00:04:09.540
So now we're trying to make archaeology in St. Croix

00:04:09.540 --> 00:04:12.160
more visible, but also bring the community in,

00:04:12.160 --> 00:04:16.000
so we can actually have them be part of the discovery.

00:04:16.000 --> 00:04:18.520
So that's why we're training local youth

00:04:18.520 --> 00:04:21.139
starting in middle school, to be the ones making

00:04:21.139 --> 00:04:24.180
the discovery with us, and help us ask questions

00:04:24.180 --> 00:04:25.700
in this space.

00:04:25.700 --> 00:04:27.780
As I'm building my lab guide for the field,

00:04:27.780 --> 00:04:29.020
they're asking questions like,

00:04:29.020 --> 00:04:31.140
"Okay what's the difference between Afrocrucian ware

00:04:31.140 --> 00:04:32.720
and a rock? What's the difference between

00:04:32.720 --> 00:04:35.740
this glass bottle and this glass bottle?

00:04:35.740 --> 00:04:37.540
Okay one's historic, one's modern.

00:04:37.540 --> 00:04:39.160
How are you making that distinction?"

00:04:39.160 --> 00:04:41.480
And then they're asking larger questions about

00:04:41.480 --> 00:04:44.400
the site, "Why did they live here and not over here?"

00:04:44.400 --> 00:04:47.340
As they're asking those questions, it's causing us to stop

00:04:47.340 --> 00:04:50.760
and think about, "Okay why would they settle here

00:04:50.760 --> 00:04:52.020
instead of over here?"

00:04:52.020 --> 00:04:54.350
So we're looking back at the maps, we're looking

00:04:54.350 --> 00:04:56.420
back at our understanding of the space

00:04:56.420 --> 00:04:58.190
and how it's changing through time, and

00:04:58.190 --> 00:04:59.978
trying to communicate to the students

00:04:59.978 --> 00:05:01.669
while the students are now, you know,

00:05:01.669 --> 00:05:04.100
pressing us— the community is pressing us

00:05:04.100 --> 00:05:06.110
to think about, "What's the next step?

00:05:06.110 --> 00:05:07.360
What can we do more of?

00:05:07.360 --> 00:05:09.700
What can we think about in a new way?"

00:05:09.700 --> 00:05:12.420
So many people from the community have been involved

00:05:12.420 --> 00:05:15.760
before we even dug the first shovel.

00:05:15.760 --> 00:05:18.160
So many people have come out in support,

00:05:18.160 --> 00:05:20.640
and shared their stories, and their photographs,

00:05:20.640 --> 00:05:21.600
and their experiences with us,

00:05:21.600 --> 00:05:24.720
that the least we can do is share the information we

00:05:24.720 --> 00:05:27.600
get from archaeology, with the community.

00:05:27.600 --> 00:05:31.400
The students who live on St. Croix that are getting a chance

00:05:31.400 --> 00:05:33.979
to do archaeology as middle school students

00:05:33.979 --> 00:05:36.960
as high school students, you know that's very unique.

00:05:36.960 --> 00:05:39.360
First of all, they're meeting their first archaeologists

00:05:39.360 --> 00:05:41.480
and getting their first taste of archaeology,

00:05:41.480 --> 00:05:44.840
but they're doing so from a team of all black scholars.

00:05:44.840 --> 00:05:47.540
who are all working at different institutions across the

00:05:47.540 --> 00:05:49.520
country, with a diverse background.

00:05:49.520 --> 00:05:51.120
There's not really an opportunity

00:05:51.120 --> 00:05:52.760
like that for most individuals.

